   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to providing reimbursement to fire companies for costs associated with responding to releases of hazardous materials",
    "basePrintNo": "S5444",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5444-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §27-0901, add §27-0927, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5444\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to providing\nreimbursement to fire companies for costs associated with responding to\nreleases of hazardous materials\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThis bill would allow municipalities or fire districts who contract\nwith, or control, fire companies to bill transportation companies for\nreimbursement when they have incurred costs in responding to\nhazardous material spills.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\nSection One defines a fire company as defined in §100(2) of the\nGeneral Municipal Law.\n\nSection Two amends the Environmental Conservation Law so that a\nmunicipal corporation or fire district who contracts with or controls\na fire company is entitled to seek reimbursement by providing for a\ncause of action when they respond to any incident that involves a\nrelease, real or threatened, of hazardous materials. Reimbursement\nshall be limited to expendable materials in the response, and be\nlimited to $10,000.\nThe state fire administrator is directed to adopt rules and\nregulations to implement the provisions of this section and to make\navailable to the municipal corporation or fire district reimbursement\nforms. The state fire administrator shall have thirty days to approve\nor deny a request for reimbursement. If approved, the municipal\ncorporation or fire district to whom reimbursement has been approved,\nshall have a cause of action to recover unpaid monies to which they\nare entitled.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nMany small municipal and volunteer fire companies accumulate\nsignificant costs in responding to hazardous material spills. Under\ncurrent law, municipal corporations or fire districts who contract\nwith control fire companies do not have the authority to bill\nhazardous material transporters for their spill response cost. This\nlegislation gives a municipal corporation or fire district a cause of\naction to recover their costs, up to a specified limit, when they\nrespond to a spill of hazardous materials.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2009-10: S.5113/A.2692A   - Passed both houses; vetoed\n6745\n2007-08: S.3834A/A.7332A  - Remained in Senate Environmental Conservation\nCommittee; Passed Assembly\n2005-06: S.891E/A.7279D   - Died in Rules; Passed Assembly\n2004:    S.7030A/A.11207A - Died in Rules\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone to the state. Benefits municipal corporations and fire districts\nby allowing them to recover costs associated with hazardous spill\nresponses.\n\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n",
